# v1.20.58 · 失落纪年 / Lost Almanac
lore.book.title=History of the Prosperous Age
lore.locked.title=Unfinished Oath
lore.locked.body=This page is bound by a royal oath. Take part in ending the Silent King's watch and earn Let the King Rest to continue the chronicle. Finding a crown or borrowing another book does not unlock it.
achievement.gtsr.silentKing=Let the King Rest
achievement.gtsr.silentKing.desc=End the Silent King's watch and unlock the great tree's final chapter.
lore.chapter.prologue.title=The Lost Almanac
lore.chapter.prologue.p0=No author signed this chronicle. A warm leaf rests inside its brass binding. The first page reads: when the world stopped answering, someone still guarded what it ought to have been.
lore.chapter.prologue.p1=The Prosperous Age carried steam into every street and ore, food and news to every city. Now the steppe sighs, the forest weeps oil, the wastes breathe dust and the swamp swallows bells. Machines left their shells; promises outlived their makers.
lore.chapter.prologue.p2=Read these pages as a traveller's testimony, not an infallible map. Biomes and ruins still hold unwritten corners. Notice relics, paths and the direction of those on watch: this land often points to its entrances in silence.
lore.chapter.steam.title=Steam and City-States
lore.chapter.steam.p0=The first city-states grew around boilers. Steam arteries crossed their workshops; coke ovens and brick furnaces turned wild ore into the rivets of city walls. Prosperity came from many stations keeping their promises.
lore.chapter.steam.p1=GTSR workshops you can build include the Large Coke Oven, Reinforced Brick Blast Furnace, Large Steam Furnace and Steam Turbine Array. These belong to the industry you can rekindle; broken machines in ruins are witnesses to its past.
lore.chapter.steam.p2=GregTech supplies the materials and industrial foundation; GTSR extends steam workshops and lost dimensions. Ruins help you understand the past. Working machines must still follow their current recipes and construction requirements.
lore.chapter.networks.title=Transport and Supply
lore.chapter.networks.p0=The old cities believed the surest royal power was a full storehouse in winter. Railways, pipes and depots lacked grandeur but formed their true skeleton. Broken sleepers lead to stations whose names have vanished.
lore.chapter.networks.p1=GTSR steam logistics clusters and hubs organize supplies; filtered cache nodes retain stock, and terminals let operators inspect their networks. Applied Energistics 2 provides an external network through the project's existing hub storage integration.
lore.chapter.networks.p2=BetterQuesting guides the mod's existing quest line, while Waila helps identify supported objects. This chronicle records the world and its people alongside those tools. Its stories remain readable without either helper mod.
lore.chapter.steppe.title=Rusted Steppe
lore.chapter.steppe.p0=The steppe once welcomed convoys of grain and ore. Wind through rusted grass sounds like a sigh repeated without end. Half-buried sleepers and plates remember trains, but no arrival bell.
lore.chapter.steppe.p1=The old name for its air was Sigh. Desolation does not mean emptiness. Low ground reveals distant remnants; the direction of an old road matters more than an isolated scrap.
lore.chapter.steppe.p2=A pane of sigh-glass resembles frozen wind. It is no key and breaks no seal. It recalls the many people who once waited here for a train home.
lore.chapter.forest.title=Gearwork Forest
lore.chapter.forest.p0=The forest's patina green once adorned artisans' badges. Trees grew through soot and pipes passed between roots; machines and plants learned to make room for each other.
lore.chapter.forest.p1=Its air carries the memory of oil. Thick crowns hide ruins; scattered pipework may reveal a workshop before its outline appears between the leaves.
lore.chapter.forest.p2=The edges of a patina work-seal are worn smooth. Its bearer once inspected the city's steam supply. Now the seal retains only the shape of responsibility. Duties that could no longer be fulfilled became posts that could never be left.
lore.chapter.wastes.title=Brass Wastes
lore.chapter.wastes.p0=The wastes were not always colourless. Furnace dust and brass fragments covered the land year by year, until dunes shone like cold fireboxes. Wind scoured the names from its factories.
lore.chapter.wastes.p1=The dusty air holds echoes of every furnace opening. Sand conceals metal debris; broken chimney lines and continuous foundations are more reliable guides than a single gleam.
lore.chapter.wastes.p2=The numbers on a brass chronicle plate are incomplete. They mark no treasure coordinates. They record generations who believed one more repair could keep their city running.
lore.chapter.swamp.title=Fumarole Swamp
lore.chapter.swamp.p0=The swamp keeps too many voices. Sunken pipes sometimes exhale steam; the surface soon grows quiet again. Its city did not fall in a single crash. It simply became harder to reach each day.
lore.chapter.swamp.p1=Miry air weighs on the horizon. Watch terraces above standing water, shallow hollows and old foundations. Swamps may border the lake, but not every pool belongs to it.
lore.chapter.swamp.p2=Mire-memory amber holds tiny plant fragments. It is no living heart, yet preserves the sunlight of one particular day. Travellers need small things that prove the world was once warm.
lore.chapter.rivers.title=Forgotten River and Withered Riverbed
lore.chapter.rivers.p0=Old accounts describe the River of Three Paths as a promise to carriers: while water flows, another wharf awaits. Dry riverbeds lay out the blank space that promise left behind.
lore.chapter.rivers.p1=Rivers and dry beds cross different lands, and their banks inherit the surrounding ground. Following them reveals ties between cities. The lake's shores speak a slower silence.
lore.chapter.rivers.p2=Notice where waterways meet ruins, but do not attribute every story to one king. The Prosperous Age held many promises and many people who never received an answer.
lore.chapter.lake.title=Forgotten Lake
lore.chapter.lake.p0=The lake swallowed the cities' reflections but spared the great tree on its island. At dawn and dusk its crown resembles an old capital held aloft, with hanging leaves for curtains that have not yet fallen.
lore.chapter.lake.p1=Its shores draw water and neighbouring lands together. The central island, great tree and court above it belong to this lake's memory. Water elsewhere cannot summon them.
lore.chapter.lake.p2=From afar the crown seems closed. Up close, paths climb its grain, rooms rest between branches and leaf curtains conceal their guards. Its treasures are not abandoned gifts. Someone still keeps account of them.
lore.chapter.seals.title=Seals and Relics
lore.chapter.seals.p0=Sealing bands protect an unfinished handover, not the box itself. Red runes repeat an old oath until those on watch can finally let go.
lore.chapter.seals.p1=Branch-room chests are bound to their own oathguards. Release every guard of a room and its seals disperse. Chests beside the throne await the king's end. Some lesser seals answer a simple touch.
lore.chapter.seals.p2=An unsealed chest may be looted or dismantled. Its shell leaves no item; remaining contents spill out. Oath fragments, broken edicts and crowns are witnesses, not universal keys to other seals.
lore.chapter.hanging_great_tree.title=The Hanging Great Tree
lore.chapter.hanging_great_tree.p0=The king refused the tallest stone palace. He planted the city's last seed on the island and said: if the earth cannot bear us, let something still willing to grow do so. Branches later hung through cloud shadows, and artisans followed their grain into the court.
lore.chapter.hanging_great_tree.p1=Royal power here was no banner on a metal platform. It was the root bearing everything. Root ribs became armrests, crown branches framed the throne and leaf curtains sheltered the guards. His majesty grew from a terrible decision: while anyone needed shelter, he would not leave.
lore.chapter.hanging_great_tree.p2=After the calamity the court bell fell silent. The king remained in the posture of his final edict. Oathguards faced branch paths and entrances where no relief would arrive. They do not attack every traveller; wound one and nearby comrades remember the same oath.
lore.chapter.hanging_great_tree.p3=To wake the king, end every watch in the tree, then approach his throne. The rising path ends at its front entrance. He accepts challengers face to face. Cross behind him after battle begins and the court will warn you; ten accumulated seconds behind him will enrage the Silent King.
lore.chapter.hanging_great_tree.p4=He rises slowly, as though every root must take up his promise again. Silence is no weakness and closeness offers no safety. Luminous circles herald his edicts; the root pulse drives those who crowd him away. Only those who end this watch may read the remaining story.
lore.chapter.king_epilogue.title=After the Crown
lore.chapter.king_epilogue.p0=When the last pulse faded, the king did not turn. It was not arrogance. He had forgotten that he too could be escorted away. The tree kept growing, for the first time without needing growth to prove a promise.
lore.chapter.king_epilogue.p1=The old crown bears no new edict. Its wounds match the grain of the roots like a nail at last drawn from timber. It testifies that this court's watch is over. The ending belongs to those who took part, not merely whoever finds the crown.
lore.chapter.king_epilogue.p2=The Prosperous Age achievement page records that you let the king rest. Seals beside the throne answer this ending; no one above waits for relief now. This chapter follows that achievement. Lending the book does not lend the deed.
lore.chapter.king_epilogue.p3=Yet the lake remains, and half the cities' names are still missing. Keep the crown and remember the paths on the roots. Perhaps the next story needs no further king, only someone willing to finish the words of the lost.
item.prosperity.history.name=History of the Prosperous Age
lore.relic.history=A brass-bound chronicle. Right-click to read; its unfinished chapter awaits the king's end.
item.prosperity.oath_fragment.name=Oath Fragment
lore.relic.oath_fragment=An oathguard's broken seal: a post may stand empty, but never its promise.
item.prosperity.old_crown.name=Old Crown
lore.relic.old_crown=First keepsake. The crown is silent; the roots remember its weight.
item.prosperity.brass_chronicle.name=Brass Chronicle Plate
lore.relic.brass_chronicle=Sand erased its date, but not the artisan's marks.
item.prosperity.patina_seal.name=Patina Work-Seal
lore.relic.patina_seal=An old steam inspector's seal, retaining the shape of duty.
item.prosperity.sigh_glass.name=Sigh-Glass
lore.relic.sigh_glass=The steppe's wind is caught in a crack.
item.prosperity.mire_memory.name=Mire-Memory Amber
lore.relic.mire_memory=Deep in the mire, a leaf keeps the sunlight of that day.
item.prosperity.royal_sap.name=Royal Sap
lore.relic.royal_sap=Jade resin from the great tree, like an unspoken promise.
item.prosperity.broken_edict.name=Broken Edict
lore.relic.broken_edict=Only the last words remain: I am still here.

